DHCP client — RFC 2131 dynamic host configuration
===================================================

When a fresh host joins a network it does not yet know its own
IP address, netmask, or default route. DHCP is the de-facto
protocol used to learn those on Ethernet-like links: the host
broadcasts a `DHCPDISCOVER`, any DHCP server on the segment
replies with a `DHCPOFFER`, the host picks one and sends back a
`DHCPREQUEST`, and the server commits by sending `DHCPACK`.

This client implements the INIT → SELECTING → REQUESTING → BOUND
arc of RFC 2131 §4.4, with DISCOVER/REQUEST retransmit per RFC
2131 §4.1 (exponential 4/8/16/32 s up to a ~60 s ceiling) so
first-packet loss on a real wire doesn't strand us. T1/T2
renewal is optional behind a `--renew` flag — default usage is
still one-shot (acquire a lease and exit) so init can pm_waitpid
for the bind, and existing tests keep their simple "run
dhclient, grep for bound line" shape.

A `--test` flag runs the parser against canned OFFER/ACK packets
so the harness can exercise this code without a live DHCP server.

BOOTP wire layout (RFC 951 §3) — 236-byte fixed header, then a
magic cookie, then variable-length DHCP options:

```
  0     1     2     3     4    ...   16    ...   28 ...  236 ... 240 ...
 [op] [hty] [hln] [hop] [   xid  ] [yiaddr] [chaddr(16)]  [cookie] [opts]
```

DHCP repurposes BOOTP's `file` and `sname` fields for overflow
options in practice, but we never reach overflow at this protocol
level. Options are encoded as `code | len | value-bytes...`, with
`code=255` (END) terminating and `code=0` (PAD) a single-byte
skip.

    module oskit.bin.dhclient

    import oskit.ulib.{puts, exit, exit_with, uptime,
        create, open, close, unlink, write_handle}
    import oskit.net.{socket, bind, sendto, recvfrom, recvfrom_timeout,
        close_socket, inet_set_ip_config, nic_get_mac,
        arp_probe_start, arp_probe_send, arp_probe_check, arp_probe_announce}
    import std.net.{AF_INET, SOCK_DGRAM, INADDR_ANY, ipv4, ipv4_octet,
        pkt_write_u16, pkt_write_ipv4}


BOOTP opcodes (`op` at offset 0). A client sends BOOTREQUEST;
the server always replies BOOTREPLY.

    val DHCP_OP_BOOTREQUEST = 1
    val DHCP_OP_BOOTREPLY   = 2


Hardware type + address length for Ethernet II. RFC 1700 assigns
type 1 to 10 Mbps Ethernet and that's been the universal value
ever since.

    val DHCP_HTYPE_ETHERNET = 1
    val DHCP_HLEN_ETHERNET  = 6


Magic cookie sits at offset 236 and marks a packet as DHCP (as
opposed to plain BOOTP). Any reply missing these four bytes in
that order is rejected outright.

    val DHCP_COOKIE_0 = 0x63
    val DHCP_COOKIE_1 = 0x82
    val DHCP_COOKIE_2 = 0x53
    val DHCP_COOKIE_3 = 0x63


The BROADCAST flag in the BOOTP `flags` field (offset 10) tells
servers to reply to the limited-broadcast address rather than
unicasting to the offered IP — which is what we need pre-lease,
since our NIC will drop any IP addressed to an address we
haven't installed yet.

    val DHCP_FLAG_BROADCAST = 0x8000


Message types carried in option 53. DISCOVER/OFFER/REQUEST/ACK
drive the happy path; NAK aborts it; DECLINE + RELEASE are
implemented by other components (address-conflict detection and
clean shutdown respectively) that we don't ship here.

    val DHCP_MSG_DISCOVER = 1
    val DHCP_MSG_OFFER    = 2
    val DHCP_MSG_REQUEST  = 3
    val DHCP_MSG_DECLINE  = 4
    val DHCP_MSG_ACK      = 5
    val DHCP_MSG_NAK      = 6
    val DHCP_MSG_RELEASE  = 7


Option codes (RFC 2132). We build options 53, 55, 12, 50, 54 and
we parse options 1, 3, 51, 53, 54, 58, 59.

    val DHCP_OPT_PAD             = 0
    val DHCP_OPT_SUBNET_MASK     = 1
    val DHCP_OPT_ROUTER          = 3
    val DHCP_OPT_DNS             = 6
    val DHCP_OPT_HOSTNAME        = 12
    val DHCP_OPT_REQUESTED_IP    = 50
    val DHCP_OPT_LEASE_TIME      = 51
    val DHCP_OPT_MSG_TYPE        = 53
    val DHCP_OPT_SERVER_ID       = 54
    val DHCP_OPT_PARAM_REQ_LIST  = 55
    val DHCP_OPT_T1              = 58
    val DHCP_OPT_T2              = 59
    val DHCP_OPT_END             = 255

    val DHCP_MAX_DNS_SERVERS = 2


UDP port numbers (RFC 2131 §4.1). Clients always speak from port
68 to server port 67.

    val DHCP_PORT_SERVER = 67
    val DHCP_PORT_CLIENT = 68


Layout offsets. 236 bytes of BOOTP header, 4 bytes of magic
cookie, options from 240 onward. 512 is our packet ceiling —
plenty for the option lists we build (~30 bytes) and for any
sane server reply.

    val DHCP_MAGIC_OFFSET   = 236
    val DHCP_OPTIONS_OFFSET = 240
    val DHCP_PACKET_SIZE    = 512


Parsed-message struct. Everything the client needs from an
OFFER/ACK, already extracted from options. `valid=1` means we
saw a well-formed packet, even if some fields are missing (a
server that doesn't advertise T1/T2 is still valid).

    struct DhcpMessage
        msg_type: int
        xid: u32
        yiaddr: u32
        server_id: u32
        mask: u32
        gw: u32
        lease: int
        t1: int
        t2: int
        dns_servers: [2]u32
        dns_count: int
        valid: int


### read_u32_be

Read a big-endian u32 from `p + off`. Used for xid, lease-time,
and IP-valued options (which are also 32-bit big-endian).

    read_u32_be(p: *byte, off: int) -> u32
        val b0 = u32(p[off]     & 0xff)
        val b1 = u32(p[off + 1] & 0xff)
        val b2 = u32(p[off + 2] & 0xff)
        val b3 = u32(p[off + 3] & 0xff)
        (b0 << 24) | (b1 << 16) | (b2 << 8) | b3


### xorshift32 PRNG

Used anywhere the RFC says "uniform random" — retransmit
jitter (RFC 2131 §4.1), ARP probe inter-packet spacing (RFC
5227 §2.1.1), DECLINE hold-off (RFC 2131 §3.1). Seeded from
uptime() in main(); non-zero seed guaranteed by or-ing 1.
Period 2^32 - 1 is plenty for the handful of bits of
randomness we draw per boot.

No cryptographic guarantees — not suitable for ISS randomization
or keying. Slix doesn't have a CSPRNG yet; when it lands, this
helper stays as a fast-path for non-security jitter.

    var rng_state: u32 = u32(1)

    rng_seed(seed: u32)
        var s = seed
        if s == u32(0)
            s = u32(1)
        rng_state = s

    rng_next() -> u32
        var s = rng_state
        s = s ^ (s << 13u32)
        s = s ^ (s >> 17u32)
        s = s ^ (s << 5u32)
        rng_state = s
        s


Uniform integer in `[lo, hi]` inclusive. `hi <= lo` returns
`lo` (no-op degenerate range — caller's fault but we don't
divide by zero).

    rng_range(lo: int, hi: int) -> int
        if hi <= lo
            return lo
        val span = u32(hi - lo + 1)
        lo + int(rng_next() % span)


### build_dhcp_packet

Zero-fill `buf`, write the BOOTP header, write the magic cookie,
then append TLV options. The caller passes our MAC for the
`chaddr` slot. `requested_ip` and `server_id` are only consulted
for REQUEST — DISCOVER leaves them at zero.

Returns the total packet length in bytes.

    build_dhcp_packet(buf: *byte, msg_type: int, xid: u32,
        our_mac: *byte, requested_ip: u32, server_id: u32) -> int
        var i = 0
        while i < DHCP_PACKET_SIZE
            buf[i] = byte(0)
            i += 1
        buf[0] = byte(DHCP_OP_BOOTREQUEST)
        buf[1] = byte(DHCP_HTYPE_ETHERNET)
        buf[2] = byte(DHCP_HLEN_ETHERNET)
        buf[3] = byte(0)
        buf[4] = byte(int(xid >> 24u32) & 0xff)
        buf[5] = byte(int(xid >> 16u32) & 0xff)
        buf[6] = byte(int(xid >> 8u32) & 0xff)
        buf[7] = byte(int(xid) & 0xff)
        pkt_write_u16(buf, 10, DHCP_FLAG_BROADCAST)
        var j = 0
        while j < 6
            buf[28 + j] = our_mac[j]
            j += 1
        buf[DHCP_MAGIC_OFFSET + 0] = byte(DHCP_COOKIE_0)
        buf[DHCP_MAGIC_OFFSET + 1] = byte(DHCP_COOKIE_1)
        buf[DHCP_MAGIC_OFFSET + 2] = byte(DHCP_COOKIE_2)
        buf[DHCP_MAGIC_OFFSET + 3] = byte(DHCP_COOKIE_3)

Message-Type (53) — always the first option per convention.

        var off = DHCP_OPTIONS_OFFSET
        buf[off] = byte(DHCP_OPT_MSG_TYPE)
        buf[off + 1] = byte(1)
        buf[off + 2] = byte(msg_type)
        off += 3

Parameter-Request-List (55) — six options we want the server
to include in its reply: subnet mask, router, lease time, T1, T2,
and DNS server list. Per RFC 2131 §3.5 the server only emits the
options the client actually asks for, so option 6 (DNS) must be
listed here to make slirp populate it on the OFFER/ACK.

        buf[off] = byte(DHCP_OPT_PARAM_REQ_LIST)
        buf[off + 1] = byte(6)
        buf[off + 2] = byte(DHCP_OPT_SUBNET_MASK)
        buf[off + 3] = byte(DHCP_OPT_ROUTER)
        buf[off + 4] = byte(DHCP_OPT_LEASE_TIME)
        buf[off + 5] = byte(DHCP_OPT_T1)
        buf[off + 6] = byte(DHCP_OPT_T2)
        buf[off + 7] = byte(DHCP_OPT_DNS)
        off += 8

Hostname (12) = "slix". Cosmetic but helps router admin UIs
label our box something useful.

        buf[off] = byte(DHCP_OPT_HOSTNAME)
        buf[off + 1] = byte(4)
        buf[off + 2] = byte('s')
        buf[off + 3] = byte('l')
        buf[off + 4] = byte('i')
        buf[off + 5] = byte('x')
        off += 6

REQUEST and DECLINE both echo back the IP the server OFFERed
(50) and the server's own identifier (54). RFC 2131 §4.3.2
requires both on REQUEST so a server can distinguish this
request from one aimed at a different server; §4.4.4 requires
both on DECLINE so the server can correlate the rejection
with the lease it just handed out.

        if msg_type == DHCP_MSG_REQUEST || msg_type == DHCP_MSG_DECLINE
            buf[off] = byte(DHCP_OPT_REQUESTED_IP)
            buf[off + 1] = byte(4)
            pkt_write_ipv4(buf, off + 2,
                ipv4_octet(requested_ip, 0), ipv4_octet(requested_ip, 1),
                ipv4_octet(requested_ip, 2), ipv4_octet(requested_ip, 3))
            off += 6
            buf[off] = byte(DHCP_OPT_SERVER_ID)
            buf[off + 1] = byte(4)
            pkt_write_ipv4(buf, off + 2,
                ipv4_octet(server_id, 0), ipv4_octet(server_id, 1),
                ipv4_octet(server_id, 2), ipv4_octet(server_id, 3))
            off += 6

End marker.

        buf[off] = byte(DHCP_OPT_END)
        off += 1
        off


### parse_dhcp_packet

Walk the options TLV starting at offset 240. Unknown codes are
skipped. Fields missing from the packet stay zero in `out`.
Caller checks `out.valid` before trusting any field — packets
that fail the BOOTREPLY / magic-cookie sanity checks land with
`valid=0` and otherwise-untouched defaults.

    parse_dhcp_packet(buf: *byte, length: int, out: *DhcpMessage)
        out.valid = 0
        out.msg_type = 0
        out.xid = u32(0)
        out.yiaddr = u32(0)
        out.server_id = u32(0)
        out.mask = u32(0)
        out.gw = u32(0)
        out.lease = 0
        out.t1 = 0
        out.t2 = 0
        out.dns_count = 0
        out.dns_servers[0] = u32(0)
        out.dns_servers[1] = u32(0)
        if length < DHCP_OPTIONS_OFFSET
            return
        if int(buf[0]) != DHCP_OP_BOOTREPLY
            return
        if int(buf[DHCP_MAGIC_OFFSET + 0]) != DHCP_COOKIE_0
            return
        if int(buf[DHCP_MAGIC_OFFSET + 1]) != DHCP_COOKIE_1
            return
        if int(buf[DHCP_MAGIC_OFFSET + 2]) != DHCP_COOKIE_2
            return
        if int(buf[DHCP_MAGIC_OFFSET + 3]) != DHCP_COOKIE_3
            return
        out.xid = read_u32_be(buf, 4)
        out.yiaddr = read_u32_be(buf, 16)
        var off = DHCP_OPTIONS_OFFSET
        while off < length
            val code = int(buf[off]) & 0xff
            if code == DHCP_OPT_END
                out.valid = 1
                return
            if code == DHCP_OPT_PAD
                off += 1
            else
                if off + 1 >= length
                    return
                val olen = int(buf[off + 1]) & 0xff
                if off + 2 + olen > length
                    return
                if code == DHCP_OPT_MSG_TYPE
                    if olen == 1
                        out.msg_type = int(buf[off + 2]) & 0xff
                if code == DHCP_OPT_SUBNET_MASK
                    if olen == 4
                        out.mask = read_u32_be(buf, off + 2)
                if code == DHCP_OPT_ROUTER
                    if olen >= 4
                        out.gw = read_u32_be(buf, off + 2)
                if code == DHCP_OPT_DNS
                    // Option 6 is a list of DNS server IPs. We
                    // keep up to DHCP_MAX_DNS_SERVERS entries
                    // and silently drop the rest — slix has no
                    // resolver yet, so two is plenty for future
                    // /etc/resolv.conf population.
                    var di = 0
                    while di < DHCP_MAX_DNS_SERVERS
                        val field_off = di * 4
                        if field_off + 4 > olen
                            di = DHCP_MAX_DNS_SERVERS
                        else
                            out.dns_servers[di] = read_u32_be(
                                buf, off + 2 + field_off)
                            out.dns_count = di + 1
                            di += 1
                if code == DHCP_OPT_LEASE_TIME
                    if olen == 4
                        out.lease = int(read_u32_be(buf, off + 2))
                if code == DHCP_OPT_SERVER_ID
                    if olen == 4
                        out.server_id = read_u32_be(buf, off + 2)
                if code == DHCP_OPT_T1
                    if olen == 4
                        out.t1 = int(read_u32_be(buf, off + 2))
                if code == DHCP_OPT_T2
                    if olen == 4
                        out.t2 = int(read_u32_be(buf, off + 2))
                off += 2 + olen
        out.valid = 1


### Output helpers

    print_udec(v: int)
        if v >= 10
            print_udec(v / 10)
        var b: [1]byte
        b[0] = byte('0' + (v % 10))
        puts(string(&b[0], 1))

    print_ipv4(ip: u32)
        print_udec(ipv4_octet(ip, 0))
        puts(".")
        print_udec(ipv4_octet(ip, 1))
        puts(".")
        print_udec(ipv4_octet(ip, 2))
        puts(".")
        print_udec(ipv4_octet(ip, 3))


### Retransmit ceiling

Initial timeout is 4 s and doubles on each retry up to a 64 s
cap, so the per-attempt schedule is 4/8/16/32 s. We attempt up
to four transmissions (DISCOVER or REQUEST), giving ~60 s total
wall-clock before giving up — inside the one-minute budget init
can reasonably tolerate during boot. RFC 2131 §4.1 also
recommends ±1 s jitter; we skip jitter for the first cut since
slix has no cryptographic / stochastic source of randomness and
a deterministic schedule collides less with other SLIX guests
sharing the same slirp segment than it would on a physical LAN.

    val DHCP_RTO_INITIAL_MS = 4000
    val DHCP_RTO_MAX_MS     = 64000
    val DHCP_MAX_ATTEMPTS   = 4


### RFC 5227 ARP-probe timing

Verbatim from §2.1.1 and §2.3. All in milliseconds, all
randomized where the RFC specifies ranges. DHCP_MAX_CONFLICTS
is not in the RFC — it's a pragmatic cap so a badly-configured
segment doesn't loop forever. After that many DECLINEs, we
bail with error.

    val ARP_PROBE_WAIT_MS       = 1000   // PROBE_WAIT
    val ARP_PROBE_NUM           = 3      // PROBE_NUM
    val ARP_PROBE_MIN_MS        = 1000   // PROBE_MIN
    val ARP_PROBE_MAX_MS        = 2000   // PROBE_MAX
    val ARP_ANNOUNCE_WAIT_MS    = 2000   // ANNOUNCE_WAIT
    val ARP_ANNOUNCE_NUM        = 2      // ANNOUNCE_NUM
    val ARP_ANNOUNCE_INTERVAL_MS = 2000  // ANNOUNCE_INTERVAL
    val DHCP_DECLINE_HOLDOFF_MS = 10000  // RFC 2131 §3.1.5 "wait 10 s"
    val DHCP_MAX_CONFLICTS      = 3


Dhclient's general-purpose "sleep for N ms" primitive: bind a
zero-byte recvfrom_timeout on the DHCP socket. If a stray
late datagram arrives during the window we consume it (with
truncation — the 16-byte scratch is smaller than a DHCP
packet) and keep waiting. Short-circuits on non-positive ms.

    dhclient_sleep_ms(fd: int, ms: int)
        if ms <= 0
            return
        val start = uptime()
        val deadline_ticks = start + (ms + 9) / 10
        while 1 == 1
            val now = uptime()
            if now >= deadline_ticks
                return
            val remain_ms = (deadline_ticks - now) * 10
            var rbuf: [16]byte
            var sip: u32 = u32(0)
            var sport: int = 0
            val got = recvfrom_timeout(fd, &rbuf[0], 16,
                &sip, &sport, remain_ms)
            if got < 0
                return
            // Either the window expired (got == 0) or a stray
            // packet landed (got > 0). Loop: the deadline check
            // at the top handles both paths uniformly.


### dhclient_probe

Full RFC 5227 §2.1.1 probe cycle against `yiaddr`:

```
PROBE_WAIT          (random 0..1 s)
3 × [ ARP probe → wait 1..2 s ]     (last wait is ANNOUNCE_WAIT = 2 s)
CHECK: any defender observed?
```

`START` arms the server-side state, each probe is sent with
`sender_ip = 0.0.0.0`, and `CHECK` reports whether any
defending ARP was observed during the window.

Returns 1 on conflict (caller should DECLINE + sleep + restart),
0 on clean (caller should announce + commit), -1 on transport
error.

    dhclient_probe(fd: int, yiaddr: u32) -> int
        if arp_probe_start(yiaddr) < 0
            return -1
        dhclient_sleep_ms(fd, rng_range(0, ARP_PROBE_WAIT_MS))
        var i = 0
        while i < ARP_PROBE_NUM
            if arp_probe_send(yiaddr) < 0
                return -1
            var gap = 0
            if i + 1 < ARP_PROBE_NUM
                gap = rng_range(ARP_PROBE_MIN_MS, ARP_PROBE_MAX_MS)
            else
                gap = ARP_ANNOUNCE_WAIT_MS
            dhclient_sleep_ms(fd, gap)
            i += 1
        val seen = arp_probe_check()
        if seen < 0
            return -1
        seen


### dhclient_announce

RFC 5227 §2.3 gratuitous announcements. Two broadcasts with
`sender_ip = target_ip = yiaddr` tell every host on the segment
that we now own the address — so any stale ARP cache entries
from a prior occupant get refreshed. Best-effort; a send
failure returns -1 but commit still proceeds.

    dhclient_announce(fd: int, yiaddr: u32) -> int
        var i = 0
        while i < ARP_ANNOUNCE_NUM
            if arp_probe_announce(yiaddr) < 0
                return -1
            if i + 1 < ARP_ANNOUNCE_NUM
                dhclient_sleep_ms(fd, ARP_ANNOUNCE_INTERVAL_MS)
            i += 1
        0


### dhclient_write_resolv_conf

Persist DHCP option 6 (DNS server list) to `/etc/resolv.conf` so
musl's `__get_resolv_conf` can resolve names against the addresses
the lease handed us. The file is rewritten on every BOUND so a
later renew with a different option-6 set replaces the previous
contents cleanly — `unlink` first, then `create` + `open` + write
each populated entry as `nameserver A.B.C.D\n`.

`unlink` is best-effort: on the first lease the file doesn't exist
yet and `unlink` returns -1, which we ignore. Failures past that
point are logged but non-fatal — DHCP itself bound, only name
resolution is impaired.

    dhclient_write_resolv_conf(ack: *DhcpMessage)
        unlink("/etc/resolv.conf")
        if create("/etc/resolv.conf", 1, 0x1FF) < 0
            puts("dhclient: resolv.conf create failed\n")
            return
        val fid = open("/etc/resolv.conf")
        if fid < 0
            puts("dhclient: resolv.conf open failed\n")
            return
        for var i = 0; i < 2; i++
            val ip = ack.dns_servers[i]
            if ip == u32(0)
                continue
            var buf: [40]byte
            var n = 0
            val prefix = "nameserver "
            val pp = *byte(prefix)
            for var j = 0; j < 11; j++
                buf[n] = pp[j]
                n += 1
            for var oct = 0; oct < 4; oct++
                val v = ipv4_octet(ip, oct)
                if v >= 100
                    buf[n] = byte('0' + (v / 100))
                    n += 1
                if v >= 10
                    buf[n] = byte('0' + ((v / 10) % 10))
                    n += 1
                buf[n] = byte('0' + (v % 10))
                n += 1
                if oct < 3
                    buf[n] = byte('.')
                    n += 1
            buf[n] = byte('\n')
            n += 1
            write_handle(fid, &buf[0], n)
        close(fid)


### dhclient_wait_match

Drain incoming datagrams until one matches our xid and the
expected message type, or the per-attempt deadline expires.
Non-matching packets (wrong xid, wrong type) are consumed and
silently ignored — mid-flight packets from a previous attempt
could still be in the pipe.

Returns:
- `1`  matched (out_msg populated)
- `0`  timeout — caller should retransmit or give up
- `-1` transport / socket error
- `-2` NAK received (only when we're waiting for ACK)

    dhclient_wait_match(fd: int, xid: u32, msg_type_want: int,
                        timeout_ms: int, out_msg: *DhcpMessage) -> int
        if timeout_ms <= 0
            return 0
        val t0 = uptime()
        val deadline = t0 + (timeout_ms + 9) / 10
        var rbuf: [512]byte
        var sip: u32 = u32(0)
        var sport: int = 0
        while 1 == 1
            val now = uptime()
            if now >= deadline
                return 0
            val remain_ticks = deadline - now
            val remain_ms = remain_ticks * 10
            val n = recvfrom_timeout(fd, &rbuf[0], 512,
                &sip, &sport, remain_ms)
            if n < 0
                return -1
            if n == 0
                return 0
            parse_dhcp_packet(&rbuf[0], n, out_msg)
            if out_msg.valid != 0
                if out_msg.xid == xid
                    if out_msg.msg_type == msg_type_want
                        return 1
                    if msg_type_want == DHCP_MSG_ACK
                        if out_msg.msg_type == DHCP_MSG_NAK
                            return -2
        0


### dhclient_exchange

Repeatedly send `msg_type_send` and wait for `msg_type_want`,
with exponential backoff per RFC 2131 §4.1. Caller handles
everything around it (packet building, state transition). Used
by both SELECTING (DISCOVER→OFFER) and REQUESTING (REQUEST→ACK).

`dst_ip` is the L3 destination — bcast for DISCOVER, usually
bcast for REQUEST, but unicast to server_id during RENEWING.

`requested_ip` / `server_id` feed into build_dhcp_packet for
REQUEST; DISCOVER leaves them zero.

Returns 0 on match, -1 on timeout-exceeded or NAK or error.

    dhclient_exchange(fd: int, our_mac: *byte, xid: u32,
                      msg_type_send: int, msg_type_want: int,
                      dst_ip: u32, requested_ip: u32,
                      server_id: u32,
                      out_msg: *DhcpMessage) -> int
        var pkt: [512]byte
        var timeout_ms = DHCP_RTO_INITIAL_MS
        var attempt = 0
        while attempt < DHCP_MAX_ATTEMPTS
            val plen = build_dhcp_packet(&pkt[0], msg_type_send,
                xid, our_mac, requested_ip, server_id)
            if sendto(fd, &pkt[0], plen, dst_ip, DHCP_PORT_SERVER) < 0
                return -1
            // RFC 2131 §4.1: randomize the retransmit wait by a
            // uniform ±1 s. Prevents lockstep collisions when
            // many clients retransmit off the same wall clock.
            var wait_ms = timeout_ms + rng_range(-1000, 1000)
            if wait_ms < 1
                wait_ms = 1
            val r = dhclient_wait_match(fd, xid, msg_type_want,
                wait_ms, out_msg)
            if r == 1
                return 0
            if r == -1
                return -1
            if r == -2
                // NAK — don't retransmit; caller handles fallback.
                return -2
            // r == 0: timed out, back off and retransmit.
            if timeout_ms * 2 > DHCP_RTO_MAX_MS
                timeout_ms = DHCP_RTO_MAX_MS
            else
                timeout_ms = timeout_ms * 2
            attempt += 1
        -1


### dhclient_run

State-machine traversal INIT → BOUND with retransmit. Returns 0
on a completed bind (out_msg holds the ACK so the caller can
enter the renewal loop), -1 on any failure.

    dhclient_run(out_ack: *DhcpMessage, out_mac: *byte,
                 out_fd: *int) -> int
        if nic_get_mac(out_mac) < 0
            puts("dhclient: nic_get_mac failed\n")
            return -1
        val fd = socket(AF_INET, SOCK_DGRAM, 0)
        if fd < 0
            puts("dhclient: socket failed\n")
            return -1
        if bind(fd, INADDR_ANY, DHCP_PORT_CLIENT) < 0
            puts("dhclient: bind :68 failed\n")
            close_socket(fd)
            return -1
        val xid = u32(uptime()) | u32(0x1000)
        val bcast = ipv4(255, 255, 255, 255)

SELECTING — DISCOVER → OFFER with retransmit.

        puts("dhclient: DISCOVER...\n")
        var offer: DhcpMessage
        val sr = dhclient_exchange(fd, out_mac, xid,
            DHCP_MSG_DISCOVER, DHCP_MSG_OFFER,
            bcast, u32(0), u32(0), &offer)
        if sr < 0
            puts("dhclient: no OFFER after retries\n")
            close_socket(fd)
            return -1
        puts("dhclient: OFFER yiaddr=")
        print_ipv4(offer.yiaddr)
        puts(" server=")
        print_ipv4(offer.server_id)
        puts("\n")

REQUESTING — REQUEST → ACK with retransmit. NAK aborts.

        val rr = dhclient_exchange(fd, out_mac, xid,
            DHCP_MSG_REQUEST, DHCP_MSG_ACK,
            bcast, offer.yiaddr, offer.server_id, out_ack)
        if rr == -2
            puts("dhclient: NAK received\n")
            close_socket(fd)
            return -1
        if rr < 0
            puts("dhclient: no ACK after retries\n")
            close_socket(fd)
            return -1

ARP PROBE — RFC 2131 §3.1.5 requires a final ARP check on the
offered address before we commit. If anyone on the segment is
already using `yiaddr` (misconfigured static host, race with
another DHCP client), we DECLINE the lease, hold off per
§3.1.5, and let the caller restart INIT. Clean window ⇒
announce and commit.

        puts("dhclient: probing ")
        print_ipv4(out_ack.yiaddr)
        puts("...\n")
        val pr = dhclient_probe(fd, out_ack.yiaddr)
        if pr < 0
            puts("dhclient: arp probe failed\n")
            close_socket(fd)
            return -1
        if pr == 1
            // Conflict — DECLINE, hold off 10 s, bail with
            // status=1 so main() can restart from INIT.
            puts("dhclient: conflict — DECLINE\n")
            var dpkt: [512]byte
            val dplen = build_dhcp_packet(&dpkt[0], DHCP_MSG_DECLINE,
                xid, out_mac, out_ack.yiaddr, out_ack.server_id)
            sendto(fd, &dpkt[0], dplen, bcast, DHCP_PORT_SERVER)
            dhclient_sleep_ms(fd, DHCP_DECLINE_HOLDOFF_MS)
            close_socket(fd)
            return 1

BOUND — install the learned lease. A server that forgot option
1 (mask) is fixed up with a /24 default; a missing router (3)
falls back to the server_id, which is usually the gateway on
consumer routers.

        var mask = out_ack.mask
        if mask == u32(0)
            mask = ipv4(255, 255, 255, 0)
        var gw = out_ack.gw
        if gw == u32(0)
            gw = out_ack.server_id
        if inet_set_ip_config(out_ack.yiaddr, mask, gw) < 0
            puts("dhclient: inet_set_ip_config failed\n")
            close_socket(fd)
            return -1
        // Announce claim per RFC 5227 §2.3 so any host with a
        // stale ARP cache for yiaddr updates. Best-effort.
        dhclient_announce(fd, out_ack.yiaddr)
        // Persist option 6 (DNS server list) so musl's name lookups
        // pick up the lease's resolvers. Errors are logged inside.
        dhclient_write_resolv_conf(out_ack)
        puts("dhclient: bound ")
        print_ipv4(out_ack.yiaddr)
        puts(" mask=")
        print_ipv4(mask)
        puts(" gw=")
        print_ipv4(gw)
        puts(" lease=")
        print_udec(out_ack.lease)
        puts("\n")
        *out_fd = fd
        0


### dhclient_renew_loop

RFC 2131 §4.4.5 renewal. Run after a successful BOUND. Sleep
until T1, then try unicast RENEWING to the server. If that
fails past T2, try broadcast REBINDING. If that fails past the
lease expiry, return 0 so the caller can restart from INIT.

Times in the ACK are seconds; we convert to 100 Hz scheduler
ticks. If the server didn't emit T1/T2, we default to T1 = 0.5
lease and T2 = 0.875 lease per RFC 2131 §4.4.5.

This function never returns on a persistent success path —
only on expiry (return 0) or a hard error (return -1). The
calling main loop is expected to restart dhclient_run on a 0
return.

    dhclient_renew_loop(fd: int, our_mac: *byte, initial: *DhcpMessage) -> int
        var ack: DhcpMessage
        ack.msg_type = initial.msg_type
        ack.xid = initial.xid
        ack.yiaddr = initial.yiaddr
        ack.server_id = initial.server_id
        ack.mask = initial.mask
        ack.gw = initial.gw
        ack.lease = initial.lease
        ack.t1 = initial.t1
        ack.t2 = initial.t2
        ack.valid = initial.valid

        while 1 == 1
            val t_bound = uptime()
            var t1_s = ack.t1
            var t2_s = ack.t2
            val lease_s = ack.lease
            if lease_s <= 0
                puts("dhclient: zero lease, stopping renew\n")
                return -1
            if t1_s <= 0
                t1_s = lease_s / 2
            if t2_s <= 0
                t2_s = (lease_s * 7) / 8
            val lease_deadline = t_bound + lease_s * 100
            val t1_deadline = t_bound + t1_s * 100
            val t2_deadline = t_bound + t2_s * 100

Sleep until T1 via a dummy recvfrom_timeout. Any unexpected
packets that arrive during this window are ignored — we check
xid later, and the server has no reason to speak to us
unsolicited while our lease is valid.

            val t_now = uptime()
            if t1_deadline > t_now
                val sleep_ms = (t1_deadline - t_now) * 10
                var rbuf: [8]byte
                var sip: u32 = u32(0)
                var sport: int = 0
                val ignore = recvfrom_timeout(fd, &rbuf[0], 8,
                    &sip, &sport, sleep_ms)

RENEWING: unicast REQUEST to server_id. Retransmit budget is
half of (T2 - T1), per RFC 2131 §4.4.5 recommendation.

            val renew_xid = u32(uptime()) | u32(0x2000)
            val unicast_dst = ack.server_id
            val now1 = uptime()
            var renew_budget_ms = 0
            if t2_deadline > now1
                renew_budget_ms = (t2_deadline - now1) * 10 / 2
            if renew_budget_ms < DHCP_RTO_INITIAL_MS
                renew_budget_ms = DHCP_RTO_INITIAL_MS

            puts("dhclient: RENEWING\n")
            var renew_ack: DhcpMessage
            val rr = dhclient_renew_once(fd, our_mac, renew_xid,
                unicast_dst, ack.yiaddr, ack.server_id,
                renew_budget_ms, &renew_ack)
            if rr == 0
                puts("dhclient: RENEW ok\n")
                ack.yiaddr = renew_ack.yiaddr
                if renew_ack.mask != u32(0)
                    ack.mask = renew_ack.mask
                if renew_ack.gw != u32(0)
                    ack.gw = renew_ack.gw
                if renew_ack.lease > 0
                    ack.lease = renew_ack.lease
                // RFC 2131 §4.4.5: if the renewing server omits
                // T1/T2, recompute from the new lease rather
                // than keeping the pre-renewal values (which
                // were anchored to an older t_bound and would
                // expire before the new lease does).
                if renew_ack.t1 > 0
                    ack.t1 = renew_ack.t1
                else
                    ack.t1 = ack.lease / 2
                if renew_ack.t2 > 0
                    ack.t2 = renew_ack.t2
                else
                    ack.t2 = (ack.lease * 7) / 8
                continue
            if rr == -2
                puts("dhclient: RENEW NAK, restarting\n")
                return 0

REBINDING: broadcast REQUEST. Budget = half of (expiry - T2).

            val rebind_xid = u32(uptime()) | u32(0x3000)
            val bcast = ipv4(255, 255, 255, 255)
            val now2 = uptime()
            var rebind_budget_ms = 0
            if lease_deadline > now2
                rebind_budget_ms = (lease_deadline - now2) * 10 / 2
            if rebind_budget_ms < DHCP_RTO_INITIAL_MS
                rebind_budget_ms = DHCP_RTO_INITIAL_MS

            puts("dhclient: REBINDING\n")
            var rebind_ack: DhcpMessage
            val br = dhclient_renew_once(fd, our_mac, rebind_xid,
                bcast, ack.yiaddr, u32(0),
                rebind_budget_ms, &rebind_ack)
            if br == 0
                puts("dhclient: REBIND ok\n")
                ack.yiaddr = rebind_ack.yiaddr
                if rebind_ack.server_id != u32(0)
                    ack.server_id = rebind_ack.server_id
                if rebind_ack.mask != u32(0)
                    ack.mask = rebind_ack.mask
                if rebind_ack.gw != u32(0)
                    ack.gw = rebind_ack.gw
                if rebind_ack.lease > 0
                    ack.lease = rebind_ack.lease
                if rebind_ack.t1 > 0
                    ack.t1 = rebind_ack.t1
                else
                    ack.t1 = ack.lease / 2
                if rebind_ack.t2 > 0
                    ack.t2 = rebind_ack.t2
                else
                    ack.t2 = (ack.lease * 7) / 8
                continue
            puts("dhclient: lease expired, restarting INIT\n")
            return 0
        -1


### dhclient_renew_once

Single RENEW or REBIND attempt with a bounded budget. REQUEST
with ciaddr = our current IP (we know it; we're bound). Unlike
SELECTING, we don't retransmit on the same exponential — we
burn through the budget with one backoff chain.

    dhclient_renew_once(fd: int, our_mac: *byte, xid: u32,
                        dst_ip: u32, our_ip: u32, server_id: u32,
                        budget_ms: int, out_msg: *DhcpMessage) -> int
        var pkt: [512]byte
        var timeout_ms = DHCP_RTO_INITIAL_MS
        val t0 = uptime()
        val deadline = t0 + (budget_ms + 9) / 10
        while 1 == 1
            val plen = build_dhcp_packet(&pkt[0], DHCP_MSG_REQUEST,
                xid, our_mac, our_ip, server_id)

RFC 2131 §3.3: during RENEWING/REBINDING the client's
`ciaddr` = current IP rather than zero. build_dhcp_packet always
zeroes yiaddr/ciaddr, so poke our_ip into offset 12.

            pkt[12] = byte(ipv4_octet(our_ip, 0))
            pkt[13] = byte(ipv4_octet(our_ip, 1))
            pkt[14] = byte(ipv4_octet(our_ip, 2))
            pkt[15] = byte(ipv4_octet(our_ip, 3))
            if sendto(fd, &pkt[0], plen, dst_ip, DHCP_PORT_SERVER) < 0
                return -1
            val now = uptime()
            if now >= deadline
                return -1
            val remain_ticks = deadline - now
            var this_wait_ms = timeout_ms
            val remain_ms = remain_ticks * 10
            if this_wait_ms > remain_ms
                this_wait_ms = remain_ms
            val r = dhclient_wait_match(fd, xid, DHCP_MSG_ACK,
                this_wait_ms, out_msg)
            if r == 1
                return 0
            if r == -1
                return -1
            if r == -2
                return -2
            if timeout_ms * 2 > DHCP_RTO_MAX_MS
                timeout_ms = DHCP_RTO_MAX_MS
            else
                timeout_ms = timeout_ms * 2
        -1


### dhclient_selftest

Drive the builder + parser against canned packets. No live
network involved — this exists so the harness can catch
byte-layout regressions without depending on a DHCP server.

Return codes track what's being checked so a failure points
straight at the broken assertion.

    dhclient_selftest() -> int
        var mac: [6]byte
        mac[0] = byte(0x52)
        mac[1] = byte(0x54)
        mac[2] = byte(0x00)
        mac[3] = byte(0x12)
        mac[4] = byte(0x34)
        mac[5] = byte(0x56)

Build a DISCOVER and spot-check the literal byte positions.

        var pkt: [512]byte
        val xid = u32(0x12345678)
        val plen = build_dhcp_packet(&pkt[0], DHCP_MSG_DISCOVER, xid,
            &mac[0], u32(0), u32(0))
        if plen < DHCP_OPTIONS_OFFSET + 3
            return 1
        if int(pkt[0]) != DHCP_OP_BOOTREQUEST
            return 2
        if int(pkt[1]) != DHCP_HTYPE_ETHERNET
            return 3
        if int(pkt[2]) != DHCP_HLEN_ETHERNET
            return 4
        if int(pkt[4]) != 0x12
            return 5
        if int(pkt[7]) != 0x78
            return 6
        if int(pkt[28]) != 0x52
            return 7
        if int(pkt[DHCP_MAGIC_OFFSET]) != DHCP_COOKIE_0
            return 8
        if int(pkt[DHCP_OPTIONS_OFFSET]) != DHCP_OPT_MSG_TYPE
            return 9
        if int(pkt[DHCP_OPTIONS_OFFSET + 2]) != DHCP_MSG_DISCOVER
            return 10

Compose a canned OFFER reply with every option we parse. Lease
= 3600 s, T1 = 1800 s, T2 = 3150 s (encoded BE 4-byte).

        var rbuf: [512]byte
        var i = 0
        while i < 512
            rbuf[i] = byte(0)
            i += 1
        rbuf[0] = byte(DHCP_OP_BOOTREPLY)
        rbuf[1] = byte(DHCP_HTYPE_ETHERNET)
        rbuf[2] = byte(DHCP_HLEN_ETHERNET)
        rbuf[4] = byte(0x12)
        rbuf[5] = byte(0x34)
        rbuf[6] = byte(0x56)
        rbuf[7] = byte(0x78)
        rbuf[16] = byte(10)
        rbuf[17] = byte(0)
        rbuf[18] = byte(2)
        rbuf[19] = byte(15)
        rbuf[DHCP_MAGIC_OFFSET + 0] = byte(DHCP_COOKIE_0)
        rbuf[DHCP_MAGIC_OFFSET + 1] = byte(DHCP_COOKIE_1)
        rbuf[DHCP_MAGIC_OFFSET + 2] = byte(DHCP_COOKIE_2)
        rbuf[DHCP_MAGIC_OFFSET + 3] = byte(DHCP_COOKIE_3)
        var o = DHCP_OPTIONS_OFFSET
        rbuf[o] = byte(DHCP_OPT_MSG_TYPE)
        rbuf[o + 1] = byte(1)
        rbuf[o + 2] = byte(DHCP_MSG_OFFER)
        o += 3
        rbuf[o] = byte(DHCP_OPT_SUBNET_MASK)
        rbuf[o + 1] = byte(4)
        rbuf[o + 2] = byte(255)
        rbuf[o + 3] = byte(255)
        rbuf[o + 4] = byte(255)
        rbuf[o + 5] = byte(0)
        o += 6
        rbuf[o] = byte(DHCP_OPT_ROUTER)
        rbuf[o + 1] = byte(4)
        rbuf[o + 2] = byte(10)
        rbuf[o + 3] = byte(0)
        rbuf[o + 4] = byte(2)
        rbuf[o + 5] = byte(2)
        o += 6
        rbuf[o] = byte(DHCP_OPT_LEASE_TIME)
        rbuf[o + 1] = byte(4)
        rbuf[o + 2] = byte(0)
        rbuf[o + 3] = byte(0)
        rbuf[o + 4] = byte(0x0e)
        rbuf[o + 5] = byte(0x10)
        o += 6
        rbuf[o] = byte(DHCP_OPT_SERVER_ID)
        rbuf[o + 1] = byte(4)
        rbuf[o + 2] = byte(10)
        rbuf[o + 3] = byte(0)
        rbuf[o + 4] = byte(2)
        rbuf[o + 5] = byte(2)
        o += 6
        rbuf[o] = byte(DHCP_OPT_T1)
        rbuf[o + 1] = byte(4)
        rbuf[o + 2] = byte(0)
        rbuf[o + 3] = byte(0)
        rbuf[o + 4] = byte(0x07)
        rbuf[o + 5] = byte(0x08)
        o += 6
        rbuf[o] = byte(DHCP_OPT_T2)
        rbuf[o + 1] = byte(4)
        rbuf[o + 2] = byte(0)
        rbuf[o + 3] = byte(0)
        rbuf[o + 4] = byte(0x0c)
        rbuf[o + 5] = byte(0x4e)
        o += 6

DNS option 6 with two servers: 1.1.1.1 and 8.8.8.8. Exercises
the list-walker in parse_dhcp_packet — if the loop dropped the
second entry or overran, asserts below would catch it.

        rbuf[o] = byte(DHCP_OPT_DNS)
        rbuf[o + 1] = byte(8)
        rbuf[o + 2] = byte(1)
        rbuf[o + 3] = byte(1)
        rbuf[o + 4] = byte(1)
        rbuf[o + 5] = byte(1)
        rbuf[o + 6] = byte(8)
        rbuf[o + 7] = byte(8)
        rbuf[o + 8] = byte(8)
        rbuf[o + 9] = byte(8)
        o += 10
        rbuf[o] = byte(DHCP_OPT_END)
        o += 1

        var msg: DhcpMessage
        parse_dhcp_packet(&rbuf[0], o, &msg)
        if msg.valid == 0
            return 11
        if msg.xid != u32(0x12345678)
            return 12
        if msg.msg_type != DHCP_MSG_OFFER
            return 13
        if msg.yiaddr != ipv4(10, 0, 2, 15)
            return 14
        if msg.mask != ipv4(255, 255, 255, 0)
            return 15
        if msg.gw != ipv4(10, 0, 2, 2)
            return 16
        if msg.server_id != ipv4(10, 0, 2, 2)
            return 17
        if msg.lease != 3600
            return 18
        if msg.t1 != 1800
            return 19
        if msg.t2 != 3150
            return 20
        if msg.dns_count != 2
            return 24
        if msg.dns_servers[0] != ipv4(1, 1, 1, 1)
            return 25
        if msg.dns_servers[1] != ipv4(8, 8, 8, 8)
            return 26

Same packet repurposed as ACK — just flip the msg-type byte.

        rbuf[DHCP_OPTIONS_OFFSET + 2] = byte(DHCP_MSG_ACK)
        var ack: DhcpMessage
        parse_dhcp_packet(&rbuf[0], o, &ack)
        if ack.valid == 0
            return 21
        if ack.msg_type != DHCP_MSG_ACK
            return 22

Malformed: smash the magic cookie and confirm valid=0.

        rbuf[DHCP_MAGIC_OFFSET] = byte(0xff)
        var bad: DhcpMessage
        parse_dhcp_packet(&rbuf[0], o, &bad)
        if bad.valid != 0
            return 23
        0


### main

`dhclient --test` runs the canned-packet selftest and prints
`dhclient: selftest ok` on pass.

`dhclient` with no argument runs the live state machine once
(INIT → BOUND) and exits. This keeps init's pm_waitpid model
simple and keeps existing tests unchanged.

`dhclient --renew` runs the live state machine, then stays in
the background handling T1/T2 renewal forever. On lease expiry
it restarts from INIT automatically.

    main(argv: []string)
        // Seed the PRNG from uptime — non-cryptographic but plenty
        // of entropy for the handful of jitter draws per boot.
        rng_seed(u32(uptime()) | u32(1))
        var renew = 0
        if len(argv) > 1
            val a = argv[1]
            if a == "--test"
                val rc = dhclient_selftest()
                if rc == 0
                    puts("dhclient: selftest ok\n")
                    exit()
                    return
                puts("dhclient: selftest failed ")
                print_udec(rc)
                puts("\n")
                exit_with(rc)
                return
            if a == "--renew"
                renew = 1
        var conflicts = 0
        while 1 == 1
            var ack: DhcpMessage
            var our_mac: [6]byte
            var fd: int = -1
            val rc = dhclient_run(&ack, &our_mac[0], &fd)
            if rc == 1
                conflicts += 1
                if conflicts > DHCP_MAX_CONFLICTS
                    puts("dhclient: too many address conflicts\n")
                    exit_with(1)
                    return
                // Loop back to INIT. dhclient_run already closed
                // the socket and slept the §3.1.5 hold-off.
                continue
            if rc < 0
                exit_with(1)
                return
            if renew == 0
                if fd >= 0
                    close_socket(fd)
                exit()
                return
            val rr = dhclient_renew_loop(fd, &our_mac[0], &ack)
            if fd >= 0
                close_socket(fd)
            if rr < 0
                exit_with(1)
                return
            // rr == 0 → lease fully expired, loop back to INIT.
